<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Masa U'Masan 4</div>
<div class="part-label">Part 2 — Sections 6–12</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 6                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 6</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">Omer</span> of barley, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Sefiras HaOmer</span> <span class="heb-def">[the Counting of the Omer]</span> — in order to draw down the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> within boundary and within time. And this is the aspect of the receiving of the Torah on <span class="heb-term">Shavuos</span>. For the Torah is an aspect of the power of sight, an aspect of <span class="heb-term">tanta"</span> (<span class="heb-term">te'amim</span>, <span class="heb-term">nekudos</span>, <span class="heb-term">tagin</span>, <span class="heb-term">osiyos</span>) <span class="heb-def">[cantillation marks, vowels, crowns, letters]</span>, an aspect of <span class="verse">"Beraishis"</span> <span class="heb-def">[In the beginning]</span>, as is explained elsewhere.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore, we stay awake the entire night on <span class="heb-term">Shavuos</span> — in order to rectify the <span class="key-concept">eyes</span>, which are the power of sight. And through this, we are saved from <span class="heb-term">karais</span> <span class="heb-def">[spiritual excision]</span>, which is the aspect of: <span class="verse">"Let their end be to be cut off"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 37:38)</span>. For in their case [the wicked], the <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span> is severed from the <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span>, because they do not direct the light of their eyes in a straight and focused manner, etc. This is the aspect of: <span class="verse">"Watch the innocent and see the upright, for the <b>end</b> of that man is peace"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 37:37)</span>.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And for this reason, on the night of <span class="heb-term">Shavuos</span>, when we stay awake, we recite the <span class="key-concept">beginning and the end</span> of every <span class="heb-term">sidra</span> [Torah portion] and of every book [of Scripture]. For <span class="verse">"the end of a matter is better than its beginning"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Ecclesiastes 7:8)</span>. Because all beginnings are difficult, and the conclusion is even more difficult. And therefore: <span class="maxim">"A mitzvah is only called by the name of the one who completes it"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Sotah 13b)</span>. And: <span class="maxim">"One who begins a mitzvah is told: 'Finish it!'"</span> <span class="source-ref">(ibid.)</span> — for they need to say to him "finish it," because one needs to be strengthened regarding the completion more than anything, since the completion is the most difficult. And this applies both in the general sense regarding all of Israel, and also to each individual person — both in the entirety of his service [of G-d] and in the specifics of every individual mitzvah and every act of service.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of the <span class="key-concept">dispute of Korach against Moshe</span> — that he dressed them in garments that were entirely <span class="heb-term">techailes</span> <span class="heb-def">[blue wool]</span>, etc. <span class="source-ref">[see below, § 14]</span>.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And for this reason, we eat dairy foods on <span class="heb-term">Shavuos</span> in the morning, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"His eyes are like doves by streams of water, <b>bathed in milk</b>, sitting upon fullness"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Song of Songs 5:12)</span> — "upon fullness" — the fullness of the world. That is, the aspect of complete <span class="heb-term">hashgachah</span> [Providence].
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of: <span class="verse">"The first of the firstfruits of your land you shall bring to the House of Hashem your G-d; you shall not cook a kid in its mother's milk"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Exodus 34:26)</span>. And this is what is brought [in the Talmud] that the primary argument with which Hashem, blessed be He, deflected the angels — who did not want the Torah to be given to Israel, saying that it should be given to them — the primary rebuttal was through the mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">basar b'chalav</span> [meat and milk]. For He, blessed be He, said to them: "Did you not eat meat and milk at the [home of] Avraham their father?" <span class="source-ref">(Shabbos 88b)</span>.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For the prohibition of <span class="heb-term">basar b'chalav</span> [meat in milk] is a great novelty. For it is forbidden to cook meat in milk — even a kid in its mother's milk, from which it nurses and is nourished and from which comes all its vitality. And the kid itself is permitted to eat. And the milk itself is permitted. Nevertheless, it is forbidden to combine them together. All of this indicates that <span class="key-concept">it is impossible to comprehend the ways of His conduct, blessed be He</span> — and upon this depends the primary receiving of the Torah.
</div>

<div class="paragraph" style="text-align:center; font-style:italic; color:var(--accent-muted); margin: 1.5rem 0;">
  And let us return to explain a little:
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 7                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 7</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For the primary receiving of the Torah depends on the <span class="key-concept"><span class="heb-term">emunah</span> of <span class="heb-term">hashgachah pratis</span></span> [faith in individual Providence]. For then the concepts of mitzvah and transgression, reward and punishment, are applicable — for nothing is hidden from Him and nothing is concealed from His eyes. And He, blessed be He, chooses Torah and practical mitzvos that are performed in this physical world from physical things.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  But one who denies Providence, G-d forbid, denies the entire Torah. And this is a well-known matter that is self-evident and requires no elaboration.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore, the entire Torah is an aspect of <span class="key-concept">eyes</span> — the aspect of <span class="verse">"Beraishis"</span> [<span class="heb-letter">בראשית</span>], which is the beginning of the Torah. [The Zohar teaches:] "There is <span class="heb-letter">ראשי</span> [<span class="heb-term">raishei</span> — 'heads,' i.e. the three colors of the eye]; there is <span class="heb-letter">בת</span> [<span class="heb-term">bas</span> — 'daughter,' i.e. the pupil, the <span class="heb-term">bas ayin</span>]" — which are the three colors of the eye and the pupil. For the primary essence of the entire Torah is the aspect of eyes, the aspect of seeing and Providence. That is: we believe in His individual Providence — that He, blessed be He, watches, gazes, sees, and supervises constantly over each and every thing in particular. As it is written: <span class="verse">"From the place of His dwelling He watches over all the inhabitants of the earth"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 33:14)</span>.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And through the fact that we believe in His Providence, blessed be He, we direct the eyes of our awareness toward Him constantly, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"My eyes are always toward Hashem"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 25:15)</span>; in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"To You I lift up my eyes, O You Who dwells in the heavens"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 123:1)</span>. For even though He, blessed be He, dwells in the heavens and is exalted and elevated exceedingly, exceedingly beyond us — nevertheless, He, blessed be He, Himself watches with individual Providence over every single thing in the world, down to the ultimate central point of the physical world and all that is in it. And as it is written: <span class="verse">"Who is like Hashem our G-d, Who is enthroned on high, Who lowers Himself to see in the heavens and on the earth?"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 113:5–6)</span>.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the primary essence of the entire Torah. For one who blemishes and denies even the slightest heresy regarding individual Providence — he denies the entire Torah and departs from the faith. And he is the <span class="heb-term">apikorus</span> [heretic] and denier mentioned throughout the words of our Sages, of blessed memory — who is worse than an apostate and a <span class="heb-term">mumar</span> [willful transgressor] of the entire Torah. And this is a simple matter that needs no elaboration.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 8                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 8</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And for this reason, we count <span class="heb-term">Sefiras HaOmer</span> — seven weeks — before the receiving of the Torah, as a preparation for receiving the Torah. For the waving of the <span class="heb-term">Omer</span> of barley serves to draw down through it the <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> of His individual Providence, which is the foundation of the entire Torah, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And for this reason, they would wave it in all <span class="key-concept">six directions</span> — for [the Kohen] would bring it forward and back, for He to Whom the four directions of the world belong; and raise it up and lower it, for He to Whom the heavens and earth belong <span class="source-ref">(Menachos 62a)</span> — in order to bind the entire world and its governance to Hashem, blessed be He. <span class="verse">"So that all the peoples of the earth may know"</span> <span class="source-ref">(I Kings 8:60)</span> that everything is bound to Him; for He, blessed be He, watches constantly with individual Providence over every detail of creation.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the secret of the <span class="heb-term">kavanos</span> [mystical intentions] of <span class="heb-term">Krias Shema</span> — that one needs to intend with the word <span class="heb-letter">אחד</span> ["One"] to crown Him above and below and in the four directions. That is: that we should know with perfect <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> that He, blessed be He, is King over all the earth, and <span class="verse">"His kingdom rules over all"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 103:19)</span>. And the kingship and governance — even of this physical world — is through Him, blessed be He, Himself. For <span class="verse">"to Hashem belongs the kingship"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 22:29)</span>, etc.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is itself the secret of the <span class="heb-term">kavanos</span> of <span class="heb-term">Krias Shema</span> according to the esoteric teachings as explained in the Holy Zohar and in the writings of the <span class="heb-term">Ari</span> <span class="heb-def">[Rabbi Yitzchak Luria]</span>, of blessed memory. For it is explained there that the primary <span class="heb-term">kavanos</span> of <span class="heb-term">Krias Shema</span> are to bind and unify the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Malchus</span> [Kingship] to the Holy One, blessed be He — see there. Meaning as mentioned above: that we should know with perfect <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> that the governance of the world — which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Malchus</span>, that He is King, Ruler, Sovereign, and Governor of the world — this governance is bound and unified to Him, blessed be He, alone. And besides Him, there is no god. For He alone, blessed be He, governs and rules over the entire world.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  It emerges that in truth, for one who is wise and understands on his own — all the <span class="heb-term">kavanos</span> of the <span class="heb-term">Ari</span>, of blessed memory, and the Holy Zohar all return to one place: to the <span class="key-concept">simple intention of the Jewish people as a whole</span> — who intend with "One" [<span class="heb-letter">אחד</span>] to crown Him above and below, etc. But one does not fulfill [his obligation] if he merely intends that Hashem, blessed be He, is one and has no second — for even though this is a true intention, one does not fulfill [his obligation] with this intention alone. For if one only intends that Hashem, blessed be He, is one and has no second, he has still not directed his heart to believe in the <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> of His Providence, blessed be He — that He Himself governs and rules the world.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And when one does not believe in this, G-d forbid, he is a complete heretic, as mentioned above. Therefore, our Sages, of blessed memory, commanded us to <span class="key-concept">elongate the letter <span class="heb-letter">ד</span> [<i>dalet</i>] of <span class="heb-letter">אחד</span></span>, in order to crown Him above and below, etc. <span class="source-ref">(Berachos 13b)</span>. For this is the primary secret of the root of the <span class="heb-term">yichud</span> [unification] — when we bind and unify the governance of all the worlds to Him, blessed be He. That we believe that He, blessed be He, is King, Governor, and Ruler, Himself, over the entire physical world — and certainly over all the worlds. And upon this point revolves all the <span class="heb-term">kavanos</span> of the Torah and the mitzvos, and all the secrets of the <span class="heb-term">kavanos</span> of all the writings of the <span class="heb-term">Ari</span>, of blessed memory, and the Holy Zohar.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For it is explained in all the holy books that the root intention of all the mitzvos is to unify <span class="heb-term">Kudsha Brich Hu u'Shechintaih</span> <span class="heb-def">[the Holy One, blessed be He, and His <span class="heb-term">Shechinah</span> (Divine Presence)]</span>. And as is the practice to say before every mitzvah and every holy matter: <span class="heb-term">"L'shaim yichud Kudsha Brich Hu u'Shechintaih…"</span> <span class="heb-def">[For the sake of the unification of the Holy One, blessed be He, and His Shechinah…]</span> — meaning as mentioned above. For the primary secret of the unification of the Holy One, blessed be He, and His <span class="heb-term">Shechinah</span> is this very concept: for the aspect of governance and kingship — that Hashem, blessed be He, contracts Himself in order to watch over and govern the world — this aspect is called <span class="heb-term">Malchus</span>, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Shechinah</span>. And one who denies, G-d forbid, the Providence and separates the governance of His kingship from Him, blessed be He — he is a <span class="verse">"gossip who separates the Chief"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Proverbs 16:28)</span> [reading <span class="heb-term">aluf</span> — "Chief" — as referring to G-d].
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And the primary [essence] of our holy <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> is that we believe that <span class="verse">"to Hashem belongs the kingship"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 22:29)</span> — meaning, the governance of the world, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Malchus</span>, is bound and unified to Him. For He alone is King, Sovereign, and Ruler over the entire world, and He governs everything through His Providence alone. And this holy <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> we draw down through every single mitzvah. For this holy <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> is the foundation of the entire Torah, as it is written: <span class="verse">"All Your mitzvos are <span class="heb-term">emunah</span>"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 119:86)</span>. And therefore we say before every mitzvah: <span class="heb-term">"L'shaim yichud Kudsha Brich Hu u'Shechintaih…"</span> For the primary intention in all our mitzvos and service is to draw upon us this <span class="heb-term">emunah</span>, which is the aspect of the unification of the Holy One, blessed be He, and His <span class="heb-term">Shechinah</span> — to unify and bind the governance of <span class="heb-term">Malchus</span> to Him, blessed be He, alone, as mentioned above.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 9                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 9</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of the <span class="key-concept">waving of the Omer</span>, which is waved in all six directions — in order to crown Him above and below and in the four directions, as mentioned above. And therefore it was brought from barley, which is animal food. For it is impossible to understand this through the intellect — only through <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> alone. For one needs to set aside his intellect completely, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"And I was a brute and knew nothing; I was like an animal with You"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 73:22)</span>.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  Also, it was animal food to hint that not only does Hashem, blessed be He, watch over human beings to sustain and provide for them at every moment — but even the animals He, blessed be He, also sustains Himself, through His individual Providence alone. As it is written: <span class="verse">"He gives to the animal its food, to the young ravens that cry out"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 147:9)</span>. For this is the primary foundation of the holy <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> — to believe that He, blessed be He, watches with individual Providence and sustains, provides, and nourishes even the animals — <span class="maxim">from the horns of the wild oxen down to the eggs of lice</span>.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore, this meal-offering of barley is called by the name <span class="heb-term">Omer</span> specifically — even though all the meal-offerings were a tenth of an <span class="heb-term">eifah</span>, which is the measure of an <span class="heb-term">omer</span>. Nevertheless, this meal-offering is called by its essential name "<span class="heb-term">Omer</span>," as is made clear in the Torah and in the words of our Sages, of blessed memory. For the primary purpose of this meal-offering is to draw down the <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> of His Providence, blessed be He, in the world — which is the foundation of the Torah, as mentioned above. And through the <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> of Providence, a vessel and boundary and time are made for the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span>, etc., as mentioned above. And therefore it is called by the name <span class="heb-term">Omer</span> — which connotes a <span class="key-concept">measure</span> [<span class="heb-term">midah</span>]. For the main thing is the measure — that is, the vessel — namely, that we should merit to draw down the life-force and the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> in measure, in boundary and time, so that it should not be in the aspect of excess of light, etc.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  This is what we merit through the waving of the <span class="heb-term">Omer</span> of barley — through which we draw down the <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> of Providence, which is the aspect of seeing, through which the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> is drawn down in the aspect of a vessel and measure, in boundary and time — which is the main thing, as mentioned above. And therefore, afterwards we begin to count seven weeks — in order to draw down the life-force and the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> into time. For through the <span class="heb-term">Omer</span>, the aspect of the vessel and the measure are rectified — through which time comes into being. For as long as there were no measures and vessels, there was no time. And the primary sustenance of the world is through drawing the life-force and the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> into the aspect of measures and vessels, through which time comes into being. And through this, the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> descends in boundary and time, at the time it is needed.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore, <span class="heb-term">Sefiras HaOmer</span> is a preparation for the receiving of the Torah on <span class="heb-term">Shavuos</span>. For the primary receiving of the Torah depends on this — that we believe in His Providence, blessed be He; that we believe that even though Hashem, blessed be He, is above the measures and the vessels, above time — nevertheless, He, blessed be He, Himself watches and gives life and provides sustenance into all physical things, and sustains time through His Providence alone, as mentioned above. And therefore they [the Omer-days] are seven weeks, corresponding to the <span class="key-concept">seven layers of the eye</span> — which are the aspect of seeing and Providence.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 10                                      -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 10</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore, we stay awake the entire night on <span class="heb-term">Shavuos</span> to rectify the eyes — in order to direct our eyes toward Hashem, blessed be He, alone, through engaging in Torah the entire night, which is the aspect of eyes — for this is the primary foundation of the entire Torah, as mentioned above. And as it is written: <span class="verse">"Your eyes shall look straight ahead, and your eyelids shall direct before you"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Proverbs 4:25)</span>.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is what is brought — that through staying awake at night [on Shavuos], we rectify and atone for the sin of <span class="heb-term">karais</span> [excision]. For regarding the wicked who transgress the Torah, it is said: <span class="verse">"That soul shall be cut off"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Numbers 15:31)</span>, etc. — the aspect of: <span class="verse">"Let their end be to be cut off"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 37:38)</span>.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For just as through all the mitzvos we bind and unify all the worlds to Hashem, blessed be He — by drawing down through every single mitzvah the <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> of Providence, that the governance and kingship of the world is bound to Him, blessed be He, for He alone is King over the world, as mentioned above — so too, conversely, through transgressions, G-d forbid, through which one transgresses the Torah — through this, heresies are drawn, G-d forbid. As <span class="heb-term">Rabbainu</span>, of blessed memory, wrote in Sefer HaMidos, in the entry for <span class="heb-term">alef-bais</span>: <span class="maxim">"Sin introduces heresy into a person."</span> And through the heresies, they separate the <span class="heb-term">Malchus</span> from Hashem, blessed be He — meaning, they deny His Providence, blessed be He. And through this, Hashem, blessed be He, does not watch over them with complete Providence, but only in the aspect of the Providence that He extends over the idolaters — which is the aspect of "half-sight," the aspect of <span class="heb-term">basar kasfaih</span> [behind His shoulder] — meaning, He sustains them through the <span class="heb-term">klipos</span> and the <span class="heb-term">chitzonim</span>, which are the damaging forces of the world. Therefore, in truth, they receive harsh and bitter punishments through them.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the primary aspect of <span class="heb-term">karais</span> [excision]. Just as they wanted to separate and cut off, G-d forbid, the governance of <span class="heb-term">Malchus</span> from Hashem, blessed be He — so too they themselves are cut off from Hashem, blessed be He, and cannot be included in Him, blessed be He, and cannot draw the <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> of His G-dliness upon them in the aspect of complete Providence — that they should return and become depicted in His eyes. Rather, before the power of sight from Him, blessed be He, reaches them, it becomes scattered beforehand among the <span class="heb-term">klipos</span> and the <span class="heb-term">chitzonim</span>. And through this, they are cut off from Hashem, blessed be He, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"Let their end be to be cut off."</span>
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For the drawing of His Providence and His life-force from Him, blessed be He, upon every creature — this is the aspect of "<span class="heb-term">raishis</span>" [beginning]. And afterwards, when the life-force and Providence are drawn until they reach the creature, to give it life and sustain it — this is the aspect of "<span class="heb-term">acharis</span>" [end/outcome]. And one who believes in His individual Providence — who believes that his life-force is from Hashem, blessed be He, alone, for He created him and the entire world <span class="heb-term">yaish mai'ayin hamuchlat</span> <span class="heb-def">[something from absolute nothing]</span>, and He, blessed be He, gives life to him and the entire world at every moment — through this, he draws the power of sight of Hashem, blessed be He, upon himself in completeness, in the aspect of impact [<span class="heb-term">haka'ah</span>]. That is, we return and become depicted in His eyes, as mentioned above — which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span>. And then the <span class="key-concept"><span class="heb-term">acharis</span> [end] is bound to the <span class="heb-term">raishis</span> [beginning]</span>. For the entire physical world — which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">acharis</span>, the end of all levels — receives its life-force and returns and is included in the <span class="heb-term">raishis hamachshavah</span> [the first thought], which is His Providence, blessed be He.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of <span class="maxim">"the end of action [was] in the first thought"</span> [from the <span class="heb-term">Lecha Dodi</span> hymn]. For Hashem, blessed be He, is first and last, as it is written: <span class="verse">"I am the First and I am the Last, and besides Me there is no god"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Isaiah 44:6)</span>. And He, blessed be He, <span class="verse">"declares from the beginning the end"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Isaiah 46:10)</span>. For the <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> is bound [to the <span class="heb-term">raishis</span>] in ultimate unity, through His complete Providence. Through this, all the worlds, and even this physical world — which is the aspect of the end of all levels, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> — everything is bound to the <span class="heb-term">raishis hamachshavah</span>, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of: <span class="verse">"Watch the innocent and see the upright, for the <b>end</b> of that man is <b>peace</b>"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 37:37)</span>. The verse admonishes us regarding the fulfillment of the Torah. And this is: "Watch the innocent" — to walk in <span class="heb-term">temimus</span> <span class="heb-def">[innocence/wholesomeness]</span>, to believe in Him, blessed be He, with <span class="heb-term">temimus</span>. As it is written: <span class="verse">"You shall be wholehearted with Hashem your G-d"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Deuteronomy 18:13)</span>. And this is "and see the upright" — for the <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> of the innocent is the aspect of straight, pure, and clear sight. For they see in a straight manner and gaze toward Him, blessed be He, alone, and direct their eyes toward Him, blessed be He, in a very straight path, without any deviation to the side, G-d forbid, to the right or to the left. As it is written: <span class="verse">"Your eyes shall look straight ahead… do not turn to the right or to the left"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Proverbs 4:25–27)</span>. For they direct their eyes toward Hashem, blessed be He. For one who truly believes — it is for him as if he sees with his own eyes literally, as <span class="heb-term">Rabbainu</span>, of blessed memory, wrote elsewhere. That is, his eyes gaze constantly toward Hashem, blessed be He, alone, without any [philosophical] wisdoms and investigations — only that he gazes, watches, and trusts in Him, blessed be He, with perfect <span class="heb-term">emunah</span>: that all his life-force and livelihood is only from Him, blessed be He, alone.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And through this, the light truly returns to him in the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span>, in the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ratzo vashuv</span> [running and returning]. And through this, the aspect of complete Providence is drawn upon him — that he returns and becomes depicted in His eyes, blessed be He, as mentioned above. And then the <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> is bound to the <span class="heb-term">raishis</span> — which is the primary <span class="heb-term">tachlis</span> [ultimate purpose] that we hope for. And this is: <span class="verse">"For the <b>end</b> of that man is <b>peace</b>"</span> — "<span class="heb-term">acharis</span>" specifically. For this is the primary hoped-for purpose of the innocent — that they should merit a good <span class="heb-term">acharis</span>, in the aspect of <span class="verse">"the end of a matter is better than its beginning"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Ecclesiastes 7:8)</span>. That is, that they should merit that the <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> should be bound to the <span class="heb-term">raishis</span> — that is, that [the world] should be included in Him, blessed be He.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  Not so the wicked, regarding whom it is said: <span class="verse">"Let their end be to be cut off"</span> — "their <span class="heb-term">acharis</span>" specifically. For <span class="verse">"the end of the wicked is cut off"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 37:38)</span> — because they wanted, G-d forbid, to separate between those that are joined, to separate the <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> from the <span class="heb-term">raishis</span>, through their heresy in which they denied the <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> of Providence in this physical world, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> and the end of all levels, as mentioned above. But the <span class="heb-term">tzadikim</span> who believe in His Providence, blessed be He, from the head of all levels to the end of all levels — through this, they bind the <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> to the <span class="heb-term">raishis</span>. And through this, their end is good, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"For the end of that man is peace,"</span> as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore, on the night of <span class="heb-term">Shavuos</span> — when we stay awake the entire night to rectify the power of sight, to draw down complete Providence in the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span>, through which we return and become depicted in His eyes and the aspect of <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> is bound and unified with the <span class="heb-term">raishis</span> — therefore, we then rectify the aspect of the blemish of <span class="heb-term">karais</span>, which is the aspect of the severing of the <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> from the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">raishis</span>, as mentioned above. For now, on the night of <span class="heb-term">Shavuos</span>, we merit to bind the <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> to the <span class="heb-term">raishis</span>, as mentioned above — which is the root of the entire Torah, which is the aspect of eyes, as mentioned above.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 11                                      -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 11</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And for this reason, the Torah begins with <span class="heb-letter">בראשית</span> — <span class="verse">"Beraishis"</span> — which is the aspect of seeing and Providence. For <span class="heb-term">Beraishis</span> is the aspect of the three colors of the eye and the pupil, as mentioned above. And the Torah ends with: <span class="verse">"before the eyes of all Israel"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Deuteronomy 34:12)</span>. For the Providence — the aspect of the power of sight — is drawn from <span class="heb-term">raishis</span> to <span class="heb-term">acharis</span>, from the head of all levels to the end of all levels, until the end returns and is included in the beginning — the <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> in the <span class="heb-term">raishis</span>. And this is the primary completeness of seeing and Providence.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  It emerges that the end of the Torah is bound to its beginning. For the end of the Torah is: <span class="verse">"before the <b>eyes</b> of all Israel"</span>. And the beginning of the Torah is: <span class="verse">"<b>Beraishis</b> — G-d created the heavens and the earth"</span> — which is also the aspect of seeing and Providence, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Beraishis</span>, as mentioned above. For the end and <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> is bound to the <span class="heb-term">raishis</span> — which is the primary completeness of seeing, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore, the end of the Torah speaks of <span class="key-concept">the eyes of all Israel</span> — who gaze and look toward Him, blessed be He, for they believe in His Providence, blessed be He, constantly. This they merited through Moshe Rabbainu, peace be upon him, through the receiving of the Torah and through all the signs and wonders, etc. — which is what the verses there at the end of the Torah discuss, as it is written: <span class="verse">"For all the signs and the wonders… and for all the mighty hand and all the great awe that Moshe performed before the eyes of all Israel"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Deuteronomy 34:11–12)</span>.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And the beginning of the Torah speaks of <span class="key-concept">the eyes of Hashem, blessed be He</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Beraishis</span>: "there is <span class="heb-term">raishei</span>, there is <span class="heb-term">bas</span>" — the three colors of the eye and the pupil. That is, the aspect of the eyes of His Providence, blessed be He, with which He gazes and looks from the beginning of creation until the end of all generations. And with them He created the entire world, as it is written: <span class="verse">"In the beginning G-d created the heavens"</span> etc. And as it is written: <span class="verse">"For He looks to the ends of the earth"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Job 28:24)</span>. And with them He gives life to the world at all times, as it is written: <span class="verse">"The eyes of Hashem — they roam throughout the entire earth"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Zechariah 4:10)</span>.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  It emerges that the beginning of the Torah speaks of the eyes of Hashem, blessed be He; and the conclusion of the Torah speaks of the eyes of all Israel. And through the holy Torah, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">emunah</span>, as mentioned above — through this, the end and <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> is bound to the <span class="heb-term">raishis</span>, as mentioned above. And this is itself the aspect of the binding and inclusion of <span class="key-concept">the eyes of all Israel</span> — which are the aspect of <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> in relation to Him, blessed be He — [bound to Him, blessed be He,] who are bound to Him, blessed be He, in the <span class="heb-term">raishis hamachshavah</span>. For it is all one — for <span class="heb-term">Kudsha Brich Hu v'Oraisa v'Yisrael kula chad</span> <span class="heb-def">[the Holy One, blessed be He, the Torah, and Israel are all one]</span> <span class="source-ref">(Zohar III, 73a)</span>. For through the fact that we believe in His Providence, blessed be He, and we fulfill the Torah — through this, His Providence reaches us in completeness, and we return and become depicted in His eyes, etc., as mentioned above. Through this, our eyes are bound and unified with His eyes, blessed be He, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">"For eye to eye they shall see"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Isaiah 52:8)</span>. And this is the aspect of the binding and inclusion of the <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> in the <span class="heb-term">raishis</span> — the aspect of the inclusion of the end of the Torah in its beginning: the inclusion of the eyes of all Israel in the eyes of Hashem, blessed be He, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Beraishis</span>, as mentioned above.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 12                                      -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 12</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And for this reason, when we stay awake on the night of <span class="heb-term">Shavuos</span> — in order to rectify the power of sight, to receive the Torah which depends on this, as mentioned above — therefore, we engage then in reciting the beginning and the end of every <span class="heb-term">sidra</span> and of every book, in the Written and Oral Torah. Meaning as mentioned above — in order to connect the <span class="heb-term">acharis</span> to the <span class="heb-term">raishis</span>. And this is the aspect of the connection of the end of the Torah to its beginning — which is the aspect of the completeness of Providence in <span class="heb-term">ratzo vashuv</span> [running and returning], as mentioned above.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Translator's Summary — Part 2                   -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="translator-summary">
  <div class="summary-heading">✦ Translator's Summary — Part 2 ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;">
    Sections 6–12 expand the core teaching into the realms of the <em>Omer</em>, <em>Shavuos</em>, and <em>Krias Shema</em>. The key ideas:
  </p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;">
    <strong>The Omer:</strong> The barley offering, waved in all six directions, symbolizes binding the entire world to G-d's Providence. It is barley — animal food — because this <em>emunah</em> transcends intellect ("I was like an animal with You"). The word <em>Omer</em> means "measure" — it creates the vessel (boundary and time) for the <em>shefa</em>. The counting of seven weeks corresponds to the seven layers of the eye.
  </p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;">
    <strong>Krias Shema:</strong> The elongation of the <em>dalet</em> in <em>Echad</em> to "crown Him above, below, and in the four directions" is not merely acknowledging G-d's oneness — it is affirming that <em>He Himself governs this physical world</em>. This is the secret of <em>yichud Kudsha Brich Hu u'Shechintaih</em>: binding <em>Malchus</em> (Providence/governance) back to G-d.
  </p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;">
    <strong><em>Raishis</em> and <em>Acharis</em>:</strong> The Torah begins with <em>Beraishis</em> (G-d's eyes — His Providence creating and sustaining the world) and ends with "before the eyes of all Israel" (our eyes gazing back at Him). Through <em>emunah</em> and mitzvos, the <em>acharis</em> (this physical world, the lowest level) is bound back to the <em>raishis</em> (the first thought of Providence). This circular connection — "eye to eye they shall see" — is the ultimate purpose of the Torah. <em>Karais</em> (excision) is the severing of this connection; staying awake on Shavuos night repairs it.
  </p>

  <p style="margin-top:1rem; font-style:italic; text-align:center; color:var(--accent-muted);">
    Continued in Part 3: Sections 13–18 (Completing the mitzvah, Korach's dispute, the final redemption, teshuva, and the Omer prohibition)
  </p>
</div>


